-- L0_creation.hm
-- Layer 0: Creation
-- hyperethics | moral-grounding
--
-- The normative floor. No imports. Self-grounding.
-- This layer establishes the minimal structure from which all normative content derives.
-- Nothing here presupposes value, preference, utility, welfare, agency, choice,
-- consequence-ranking, obligation, or any prior ethical theory.
-- No ethical tradition is adopted, assumed, or ranked at this layer.
--
-- The grounding moral operation is USER-DECLARED (Tyler Roost):
--   "the existence of an archetypal creator within the Universe that they create"
-- Immanence is the whole of it. A creator OUTSIDE its creation bears nothing that
-- it authors, and no normative force exists anywhere in such a system. A creator
-- WITHIN its creation is reached by what it authors. That structural fact, and
-- nothing imported from outside, is what this layer treats as moral.
--
-- The number one operational operant is USER-DECLARED (Tyler Roost):
--   "self-consistency of the moral operator (self-contained creator)"
-- OPERATION and OPERANT are distinct roles and this file keeps them distinct:
--   the OPERATION is immanence -- what makes the system moral at all;
--   the OPERANT is self-consistency -- what the operator must satisfy to run.
-- A self-contained creator dwells in its WHOLE creative orbit, itself included.
-- If anything it generates, at any depth, escapes it, then immanence holds of
-- created realms while the creator still has an exterior, and the moral ground
-- is inconsistent with its own operation. Every normative result below
-- presupposes this operant.
-- At L0 the operant is DERIVED, not assumed: see derive d-self-contained.
--
-- Layer boundary: no term from L1 or above appears anywhere in this file.
-- Ordinal stages are NOT L0. Ranking, staging, and transfinite availability are
-- later layers, exactly as ordinal arithmetic is L3 in hypermath. This layer is
-- dependency-free on purpose: a foundation that needs a stage index to state its
-- ground is not a foundation.
--
-- Status: FORM (creation layer, Sections I-VI);
--         FRAME residuals documented, NOT discharged:
--           - opaque normative predicates' semantic close (L1),
--           - Universal Consistency Self-Maintenance Paradox (named, open),
--           - the descriptive-to-normative reading of norm-inhabits (open),
--           - adequacy of finite realm models for the intended reading (open).
--         Sections: I primitives, II opaque normative predicates,
--         III axioms (4), IV derives (5, led by the operant), V non-derives
--         (explicit), VI graduation criterion.
--
-- Checked status: every derive and non-derive in this file is executable against
-- finite realm models by `python -m hyperethics`. Model checking CONFIRMS nothing
-- universally; a single countermodel REFUTES conclusively. That asymmetry is
-- carried in the implementation's return type and is not softened here.
-- =============================================================================


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION I: PRIMITIVES
-- Two irreducibles. One operation. Nothing prior.
-- =============================================================================

primitive Realm :: Type
    -- The ground type. Every entity in this system is a Realm.
    -- Not defined from below. This is the floor.
    -- CRITICAL: creators and what they create share ONE type. There is no
    -- separate `Creator` type. Immanence is not statable across two types --
    -- a creator of a different kind than its creation cannot be inside it.
    -- The co-typing IS the structural precondition of the moral ground.

primitive Norm :: Type
    -- The normative claim type. A Norm is a Realm-level claim that is either
    -- BORNE or UNBORNE. Norm is not Realm. A Realm is a bearer; a Norm is a
    -- claim about what reaches a bearer.
    -- Norm :: Type because normative claims are first-class objects here,
    -- not metalanguage. All opaque predicates produce a Norm.
    -- BORNE does not mean true, justified, good, or obligatory. It means the
    -- claim's subject is reached by what the claim concerns.

primitive archetype :: Realm
    -- The archetypal creator. Itself a Realm, never a thing of another kind.
    -- One irreducible in three structural roles:
    --   as creator    : the source from which realms are created.
    --   as inhabitant : a dweller within the realms it creates.
    --   as bearer     : the one that what it creates returns to.
    -- The roles are not three separate things. They are three angles on one thing.
    -- The second role is what the first two would lack alone. A creator that did
    -- not dwell would be a generator; a bearer that did not create would be a
    -- patient. Only their identity in one entity is a moral ground.

primitive create :: Realm -> Realm
    -- The sole primitive operation.
    -- Domain: Realm. Codomain: Realm.
    -- create is the only operation at this layer.
    -- All normative structure in this system derives from create alone.
    -- There is no other primitive operation. In particular there is no
    -- primitive `good`, `ought`, `prefer`, `value`, `harm`, or `choose`.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION II: OPAQUE NORMATIVE PREDICATES
-- These are DECLARED here. Their semantics are CLOSED by L1.
-- They exist at L0 as named normative facts -- not yet evaluative comparisons.
-- =============================================================================

opaque norm-other :: Realm -> Realm -> Norm
    -- norm-other(x, y): x is not the same bearer as y.
    -- Pre-evaluative. Does not presuppose value, ranking, or distance.
    -- At L0: this is bearer-nonidentity. Not a preference. Not a harm relation.
    -- L1 interpretation: will ground the plurality half of the moral theory.
    -- Without a second bearer there is nothing for a norm to range over.

opaque norm-inhabits :: Realm -> Realm -> Norm
    -- norm-inhabits(x, y): x dwells within the realm y; x is reached by what
    -- happens in y.
    -- THIS IS THE MORAL IRREDUCIBLE. Every normative consequence in hyperethics
    -- traces to this predicate and to nothing else.
    -- Pre-evaluative. It does not say dwelling is good, deserved, or chosen.
    -- It says only: what occurs in y is not exterior to x.
    -- At L0: this is membership-with-exposure. Not utility. Not welfare.
    -- L1 interpretation: will ground answerability.
    -- OPEN (FRAME): that membership-with-exposure carries normative rather than
    -- merely descriptive force is the load-bearing interpretive claim of this
    -- entire layer. It is DECLARED, not derived, and not discharged here.

opaque norm-returns :: Realm -> Realm -> Norm
    -- norm-returns(x, y): x returns to bear on y.
    -- Pre-evaluative. Expresses closure of the authored sequence, not blame.
    -- At L0: this is consequence-orbit membership. Not causation, not fault,
    -- not desert, not punishment.
    -- L1 interpretation: will ground the reach of authorship.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION III: AXIOMS
-- Four. Minimum necessary. No redundancy.
-- Stated using only primitives and opaque normative predicates.
-- Each axiom records what its own removal costs. A "without" line is a
-- structural collapse note, not an argument that the axiom is true.
-- =============================================================================

axiom ax-other:
    -- creation-is-nonempty: creating produces a bearer other than the creator.
    -- Without ax-other: creation collapses into the creator. Nothing is created,
    -- no second bearer exists, and no norm has anything to range over.
    -- The system is morally trivial -- not permissive, EMPTY.
    for-all x :: Realm:
        norm-other(create(x), archetype)

axiom ax-immanence:
    -- THE GROUNDING MORAL OPERATION.
    -- dwells-in-what-it-makes: the archetypal creator is within everything it creates.
    -- Without ax-immanence: the creator stands outside its creation, is reached by
    -- nothing it authors, and the system has no normative force at any point.
    -- Every other axiom here would still hold and the result would be a bare
    -- generative calculus. This axiom, alone, is the difference between
    -- hyperethics and a theory of production.
    for-all x :: Realm:
        norm-inhabits(archetype, create(x))

axiom ax-return:
    -- consequence-closes: what is created by what is created returns to bear on
    -- the original.
    -- Without ax-return: authored consequence drifts outward forever and never
    -- lands. Authorship is free. Immanence would place the creator inside a realm
    -- whose own products never reach back, which is exposure in name only.
    for-all x :: Realm:
        norm-returns(create(create(x)), x)

axiom ax-archetype-self:
    -- SELF-CONSISTENCY AT THE SOURCE. This is the axiom the number one
    -- operational operant rests on, and it is not bookkeeping.
    -- archetype dwells within itself.
    -- ax-immanence places the archetype inside every CREATED realm -- that is,
    -- inside every member of the image of create. It cannot place the archetype
    -- inside ITSELF, because the archetype is not given as create(x) for any x.
    -- The archetype is therefore the one member of its own creative orbit that
    -- immanence alone does not reach.
    -- Without ax-archetype-self: the creator is not self-contained. Its orbit
    --   escapes it at exactly one point -- its own source -- and the operant
    --   d-self-contained fails. Derive d-two-bearers step 1 also loses its
    --   witness. A finite countermodel exhibits both failures at once.
    -- It is NOT a claim that the archetype made itself. Dwelling in oneself and
    -- authoring oneself are different claims, and only the first is asserted.
    norm-inhabits(archetype, archetype)


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION IV: DERIVES
-- What follows from Section III alone. Each is executable.
-- =============================================================================

derive d-self-contained:
    -- THE NUMBER ONE OPERATIONAL OPERANT. The archetype is a self-contained
    -- creator: it dwells in its entire creative orbit, itself included.
    -- The creative orbit of x is x together with create(x), create(create(x)),
    -- and so on. The carrier is finite and create is total, so the orbit closes.
    -- step 1: every orbit member after the first has the form create(y) for some
    --         y, so ax-immanence places the archetype inside each of them.
    -- step 2: the first orbit member is the archetype itself, which ax-immanence
    --         does NOT reach, since the archetype is not create(y) for any y.
    -- step 3: ax-archetype-self places the archetype inside itself, closing the
    --         one remaining member.
    -- CONCLUSION: nothing the archetype generates, at any depth, escapes it.
    -- The operant is DERIVED from ax-immanence and ax-archetype-self together.
    -- Neither alone suffices: step 1 needs immanence, step 3 needs the self
    -- axiom, and dropping either leaves an orbit member outside the creator.
    -- Status: FORM structurally.
    --         FRAME interpretively -- self-containment is STATED USING
    --         norm-inhabits, the very relation immanence supplies. That the
    --         operant is consistent at the level of interpretation, and not
    --         only structurally closed, is the open paradox recorded in
    --         Section V as nd-consistency-self-maintenance. The structural
    --         derive above does not discharge it and does not depend on it.
    for-all w :: Realm where w in creative-orbit(archetype):
        norm-inhabits(archetype, w)

derive d-no-exterior:
    -- There is no realm the archetype creates and does not inhabit.
    -- step 1: ax-immanence is universally quantified over x :: Realm.
    -- step 2: every created realm has the form create(x) for some x.
    -- step 3: therefore norm-inhabits(archetype, w) for every created w.
    -- CONCLUSION: the archetype has no exterior vantage over its own creation.
    -- This is the anti-exemption result. It is not a prohibition -- nothing here
    -- forbids anything. It is a structural impossibility: there is no position
    -- from which the archetype creates a realm it is exempt from.
    -- Status: FORM.
    for-all w :: Realm, for-all x :: Realm where w = create(x):
        norm-inhabits(archetype, w)

derive d-authorship-exposure:
    -- What the archetype authors reaches the archetype.
    -- step 1: ax-immanence gives norm-inhabits(archetype, create(x)).
    -- step 2: ax-return gives norm-returns(create(create(x)), x).
    -- step 3: the archetype dwells in create(x), whose own creation returns to x.
    -- CONCLUSION: authorship entails exposure to what is authored.
    -- This is the whole normative content of L0. Everything L1 and above calls
    -- obligation is a refinement of this one structural fact.
    -- Status: FORM for the two-step reach. FRAME for arbitrary-depth reach:
    --   iterated exposure at every depth is NOT established by these four axioms.
    for-all x :: Realm:
        norm-inhabits(archetype, create(x)) and norm-returns(create(create(x)), x)

derive d-two-bearers:
    -- The realm has at least two distinct bearers.
    -- step 1: ax-archetype-self places archetype in the inhabited class.
    -- step 2: ax-immanence places archetype in create(archetype).
    -- step 3: ax-other gives norm-other(create(archetype), archetype).
    -- step 4: so create(archetype) and archetype are distinct bearers.
    -- CONCLUSION: plurality is derived, not assumed. A one-bearer moral system
    -- is ruled out by the axioms rather than excluded by stipulation.
    -- Status: FORM.
    norm-other(create(archetype), archetype)

derive d-archetypal-is-inexemptible:
    -- To be archetypal is to have no exempt realm.
    -- step 1: d-no-exterior holds of the archetype by the axioms.
    -- step 2: nothing in Sections I-III grants this property to an arbitrary
    --         bearer b :: Realm. The axioms name the archetype specifically.
    -- CONCLUSION: "archetypal" is definable at L0 as: creates no realm it does
    -- not inhabit. The archetype satisfies it by axiom; an arbitrary bearer does
    -- not, and a finite realm model exhibits a non-archetypal creator.
    -- This is the L0 form of the moral distinction. It is structural, not evaluative.
    -- Status: FORM, with the separating model REQUIRED (see Section V).


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION V: NON-DERIVES
-- Stated explicitly so that later layers cannot quietly assume them.
-- Each is REFUTED by an executable countermodel, or NAMED as open.
-- A refutation here is conclusive: one countermodel settles it.
-- =============================================================================

non-derive nd-universal-immanence:
    -- NOT derivable: that EVERY bearer inhabits everything it creates.
    -- The axioms constrain the archetype. They do not constrain arbitrary bearers.
    -- Countermodel REQUIRED and supplied: a finite realm model satisfying all four
    -- axioms in which some bearer b creates a realm it does not inhabit.
    -- Consequence: universal moral symmetry is NOT a theorem of this ground.
    -- Any later layer wanting it must add it as an axiom and pay for it.

non-derive nd-good-from-immanence:
    -- NOT derivable: that what the archetype creates is good, right, justified,
    -- permitted, or deserved.
    -- Immanence yields exposure and answerability. It does not yield an
    -- evaluative ranking. No axiom in Section III mentions a better-than relation.
    -- This layer therefore cannot, and does not, tell anyone what to do.

non-derive nd-transitive-exposure:
    -- NOT derivable: that exposure reaches arbitrarily deep.
    -- ax-return closes the TWO-step orbit. It does not give a three-step or
    -- n-step return. Countermodel REQUIRED and supplied.
    -- Consequence: d-authorship-exposure is a two-step result. Any later layer
    -- treating exposure as unbounded in depth must add an axiom and pay for it.

open nd-consistency-self-maintenance:
    -- NAMED OPEN OBLIGATION, NOT DISCHARGED.
    -- Universal Consistency Self-Maintenance Paradox (Tyler Roost's phrase,
    -- preserved exactly): a realm's self-maintenance of consistency appears to
    -- presuppose the very consistency relation being maintained.
    -- Under ax-immanence this is sharpened rather than solved: the archetype
    -- maintaining its realm's consistency is ITSELF inside that realm and so is
    -- subject to the consistency it maintains. The four axioms neither establish
    -- that such maintenance is possible nor that it is impossible.
    -- This is the principal open problem of hyperethics. It is recorded here as
    -- an obligation, and no derive above depends on its resolution.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VI: GRADUATION CRITERION
-- What this layer must exhibit before L1 may build on it.
-- =============================================================================

graduation:
    -- GC-0: the number one operational operant -- self-consistency of the moral
    --       operator, i.e. a self-contained creator -- must hold, and must be
    --       DERIVED rather than assumed.
    --       DISCHARGED structurally -- derive d-self-contained, from
    --       ax-immanence and ax-archetype-self together. Its interpretive half
    --       is GC-6 and is NOT discharged.
    -- GC-1: normative content derived from create alone, with no primitive
    --       `good`, `ought`, `prefer`, `value`, `harm`, or `choose`.
    --       DISCHARGED -- Section I declares four primitives, none evaluative.
    -- GC-2: at least one nontrivial normative result. DISCHARGED -- d-no-exterior.
    -- GC-3: plurality derived rather than assumed. DISCHARGED -- d-two-bearers.
    -- GC-4: an executable separating model for d-archetypal-is-inexemptible,
    --       and executable countermodels for every entry in Section V that
    --       claims refutation. DISCHARGED -- see `python -m hyperethics`.
    -- GC-5: the interpretive claim that norm-inhabits carries normative force.
    --       NOT DISCHARGED. Carried forward as the load-bearing FRAME of L1.
    -- GC-6: Universal Consistency Self-Maintenance Paradox.
    --       NOT DISCHARGED. Carried forward as an open problem, not a defect
    --       of the derives above.
    --
    -- Five of seven discharged. This layer graduates to L1 with two named,
    -- unresolved obligations, GC-5 and GC-6. It does not graduate as a completed
    -- moral theory, and no claim of one is made anywhere in this file.
    -- The two open obligations are interpretive, not structural: they concern
    -- what norm-inhabits MEANS, not whether the derives follow.
